                    coach_r1_late:          `<strong>Waiting for the facts to be “fully in” is how rumors win.</strong> Intervention is most effective <em>early</em>, while reach is still doubling. Wait for the peak and you're rebutting common knowledge — by then no certificate dislodges it.`,
                    coach_r1_price:         `<strong>Cutting the price treats a trust problem as a discount problem.</strong> Shoppers aren't bouncing because it's expensive; they're bouncing because the page now reads as risky. A discount on a distrusted listing signals you're clearing tainted stock.`,
                    coach_r1_rumoronly:     `<strong>You've missed the compounding mechanic.</strong> The marketplace algorithm reacted to the rating drop and quietly demoted the listing — so a sentiment problem became a visibility problem that bleeds revenue independent of the rumor. Name it now or you'll be fighting an invisible second front.`,
                    coach_r1_slow:          `<strong>Intervening this late lets the rumor calcify.</strong> Past the 72-hour window the post stops being news and starts being “what everyone knows.” The doubling curve has already done its work — every hour now is reach you can't claw back.`,
                    coach_r2_empathy:       `<strong>Empathetic, evidence-based engagement is the modelled move.</strong> You answered the worried customers <em>and</em> published the lab certificate and batch traceability — reducing the ambiguity that drove the anxiety. Sentiment climbs and the rumor loses its oxygen, because you addressed the audience, not just the accuser.`,
                    coach_r2_rebuttal:      `<strong>The certificate helps — but a bare rebuttal leaves the worried unaddressed.</strong> Facts resolve the ambiguity; empathy keeps the genuinely anxious buyers from feeling dismissed. Pair the evidence with a direct, human answer and the same certificate does far more work.`,
                    coach_r2_legal:         `<strong>“Baseless… we reserve all legal rights” answered the attacker and ignored the audience.</strong> The genuinely worried customers now feel dismissed, and dozens post that the brand “lawyered up instead of answering.” Legalism amplifies the rumor for an ambiguous scare. Who were you actually trying to convince?`,
                    coach_r2_silence:       `<strong>You chose silence.</strong> By day six reach hit 4.8 million, the journalist published “Tulsi did not respond to requests for comment,” and the marketplace added the precautionary banner. Silence didn't let it die — it let the rumor become the only story. When does waiting stop being patience and start being negligence?`,
                    coach_r2_fake:          `<strong>You ran a paid 5-star drive and 600 reviews appeared in 48 hours.</strong> The fraud-detection system flagged the velocity spike, purged the reviews, and suspended the listing for seven days pending audit. You converted a rumor crisis into a policy violation — and handed the accuser proof that you game reviews. Was the moat worth burning to defend it?`,
                    coach_r2_quietfakes:    `<strong>“A few quiet seeded reviews” is still astroturfing.</strong> Marketplace fraud detection reads velocity and patterns, not your intentions — and the original accuser is watching for exactly this. A little fake is the whole moat at risk for a marginal, deniable gain.`,
                    coach_r2_genuine:       `<strong>That's the moat working <em>for</em> you.</strong> A verified-buyer drive and real micro-advocates put authentic 4.6-star voices back on the page — the highest-trust, lowest-cost signal there is — and it's exactly what your empathetic posture earns the right to ask for. Review velocity turns positive without a single fake.`,
                    coach_r2_genuine_blocked:`<strong>Right play, wrong moment.</strong> You're asking genuine customers to advocate — but in Round 1 you branded the whole wave a smear, so the advocates feel you don't trust <em>them</em> either. Authentic WOM only mobilizes for a brand that treated its real customers as allies first.`,
                    coach_r2_womnone:       `<strong>The statement alone leaves the loudest WOM unchallenged.</strong> Sudhir's finding is that customers default to whatever word of mouth is loudest — right now that's the pile-on. Without genuine voices amplified, your evidence sits on a page the rumor still owns.`,
                    coach_r3_paidearly:     `<strong>You poured budget into a page customers still distrust.</strong> Impressions are up, but shoppers see the ad, read the 1-star pile-on, and bounce — conversion barely moves and your ROI is stuck below 1×. You're paying to drive traffic to a page that converts skeptics into non-buyers. Repair first, then amplify.`,
                    coach_r3_paidgood:      `<strong>Now the spend works.</strong> Because sentiment was already repaired, the paid traffic lands on a page that converts — visibility you defend is visibility worth having. This is the sequence: trust first, amplification second.`,
                    coach_r3_after:         `<strong>Sequencing it correctly.</strong> Repair the page, then scale paid against a listing that converts. That ordering is the single biggest ROI lever in the whole crisis — and it's the one most teams get backwards.`,
                    coach_r3_now:           `<strong>“Defend visibility now” is the expensive instinct.</strong> Forcing the demoted listing up the rankings before sentiment recovers just shows the ad to more skeptics. Visibility on a distrusted page is traffic you pay for and then lose.`,
                    coach_r3_never:         `<strong>Zero paid is over-correcting.</strong> Paid media isn't the villain — paid media <em>before repair</em> is. Once the page converts, refusing to defend the demoted listing leaves the algorithm's burial in place and cedes visibility you've earned the right to reclaim.`,
                    coach_r3_balanced:      `<strong>That's the allocation leadership can defend.</strong> The repair lines — advocates, community, PR and the lab landing page — carry the weight, paid stays measured until the page converts, and you've kept a reserve for a second wave. ROI clears 1× because every rupee lands on a page that's been made worth visiting.`,
                    coach_r4_document:      `<strong>You took the banner off the table.</strong> A documented response inside the five-day deadline, plus the journalist's balanced piece quoting you, gives the category managers exactly the cover they needed — no precautionary banner, and the second wave loses its foothold.`,
                    coach_r4_panic:         `<strong>Chasing the rumor onto every platform at once is motion, not strategy.</strong> You spread a thin response across channels instead of bending the curve where it lives, and the second wave gets room to breathe. Re-balance toward what's working; don't abandon the plan the moment it's tested.`,
                    coach_r4_deny:          `<strong>Refusing to document is how the banner goes up.</strong> Telling the category manager “it's all fake” without evidence, again treating real customers as enemies, hands the marketplace its reason to act — the precautionary banner lands and a second wave reignites.`,
                    coach_r4_moreads:       `<strong>More ads on a page that still doesn't convert is the same mistake, louder.</strong> Visibility isn't the deliverable — conversion is. Until sentiment is repaired, every extra rupee of paid media is ROI you're setting on fire.`,
                    coach_r4_freeze:        `<strong>Freezing the budget ignores the week-2 signal.</strong> The whole point of kill/scale triggers is to move money toward what's repairing the page and away from what isn't. Standing pat is a decision not to learn from the data you fought to get.`,
                    coach_r4_system:        `<strong>That's the resilience the brand needed before all this.</strong> Velocity monitoring catches the next rumor in its first 12 hours, the advocate program keeps genuine WOM flowing, and the rapid-response protocol means you never start a crisis at hour 72 again. You've turned an incident into an immune system.`,
                    coach_r4_noplaybook:    `<strong>No playbook means the next rumor starts at hour 72 too.</strong> The lesson of this crisis is that detection and a standing advocate program would have caught it in 12 hours. Walk away without a system and you've paid the tuition without keeping the lesson.`,
                    coach_r4_scorecard:     `<strong>That's a pitch a CEO trusts.</strong> Four honest KPIs — sentiment recovered, reach contained, conversion climbing, ROI above 1× — plus the net revenue saved. You didn't headline a vanity metric or blame the algorithm; you showed the curve bending and what bent it.`,
                    coach_r4_vanity:        `<strong>Leading with impressions is leading with the vanity number.</strong> Reach was the <em>problem</em>, not the win. A CEO will ask what the impressions converted to — and if the answer isn't sentiment, conversion and ROI, the headline collapses.`,
                    coach_r4_blame:         `<strong>Blaming the algorithm and the troll abdicates the job.</strong> The demotion and the rumor were the conditions you were hired to manage. Boards don't fund excuses; they fund the leader who shows the four numbers and owns what moved them.`,

